#### Testing with ArrayList with parameterized PexSamples

Below is a simple example of how you might use the PexSamples functionality to add a variety of sample ArrayList inputs.

```csharp
using System.Collections;
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    [TestClass]
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        [TestMethod]
        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_AddRange_ArrayList_Contract([PexSampleValues(10, ParameterType.Parameter)] ArrayList arrList, [PexSampleValues(10, ParameterType.Parameter)] int[] items)
        {
            for (int i = 0; i < items.Length; i++)
            {
                arrList.Add(items[i]);
            }
            
            for (int i = 0; i < items.Length; i++)
            {
                Assert.IsTrue(arrList.Contains(items[i]));
            }
        }
    }
}
```
This test runs multiple test cases using various inputs values.  The [PexSampleValues] attribute generates a variety of sample input data for the test method's parameters. 

#### Testing a complex property or method

Some methods or properties can't be unit tested because their behavior is dependent on many external factors. Testing such methods would involve coupling many components together which is too complex and makes for a poor unit test. Sometimes such methods can be tested in an integration test which tests the interaction of the system under test with its environment e.g. a database, an API, etc.

### Testing guidelines

- Unit Tests should be independent and should run quickly.
- All unit tests should share the same codebase.
- A class can have just one test class.
- Make use of Arrange, Act, Assert (AAA) methodology.
- A test method should test only one thing.
- Don't test uncode (private methods).
- Tests should be able to run in any order.
- If a class has a "Save" method, then a unit test might test the following scenarios. It should verify that, when the Save method is called, the data is written to the database correctly, that Save throws an exception with an appropriate message when attempting to save invalid data, and that Save overwrites existing data when it is called multiple times.
- Avoid use of a "magic" number (any arbitrary unique number or string that can't be identified as a bug due to its arbitrary value), instead, use meaningful names for constants.
- Test non-functional requirements too, i.e., performance, maintainability, reliability, usability requirements, and any requirements specified in the vision or features of the system.
- If a particular system functionality can be tested by different types of test (e.g., black box testing, white box testing), choose the one(s) that best fit the function being tested. 
- Be sure to test all normal and abnormal path of your code.
- If the class under test has any IO (like file system, database, a network, etc.), make sure to isolate it because it makes the unit testing very complex and error-prune.
- A test should not read the hard-coded values, it should have its own values to validate.

### Further learning for Unit Testing

- Martin Fowler's Article on Unit testing
- Clean Code: Robert C. Martin 
- Testable Code: Michael Feathers
- Agile Software Testing: Alan Jacobs, Tom Read 
- Fundamentals of Software Testing: Narasimha Karri 
- The Art of Software Testing: Paul Ammann

Source: [Unit Testing in .NET](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/testing/)--------------------            // this can help with proving the pre- and post-conditions hold
