The first step when examining this test case is to understand the goal of the test. This test is trying to verify the behavior of an ArrayList's IndexOf method. The ArrayList type includes the IndexOf method, which is used to get the index of the first occurrence of an item in the ArrayList.

### Understanding the State of the Test
To understand how we have structured this test, we need to examine each section of this test and what each one is designed to test.

The assumption section tests initial conditions using an array list and an integer x. This section ensures the ArrayList and the integer exist and hold valid information before the method is being tested.

In the action section, we see the Invoke call to ArrayList.IndexOf() which is the method we are testing. Here we are looking for the first occurrence of an item in the array.

The new state section shows the ArrayList after IndexOf() has been invoked. We are looking at the count of the array list and the last index of x in the array list, whether the array list contains x, and the return of the IndexOf().

The PostCondition section uses an Assert.IsTrue() method which checks if IndexOf() returns a valid result.

### Testing the IndexOf() functionality
The IndexOf() method is used to get the index of the first occurrence of an item in the ArrayList. When we use an ArrayList object with some data, the method returns the first index where the item is found. If the item does not exist in the ArrayList, -1 is returned.

The IndexOf() method does not alter the original ArrayList but returns the position at which the specified element was found.

This test verifies this functionality by observing the state of the ArrayList before and after calling the IndexOf() method and comparing the results.

### Testing for Error
This test is designed to verify that IndexOf() method returns the correct index. An assertion error will be thrown if the IndexOf() calculation does not match the expected value.

### Adding Test Data and Running the Test
Before writing the test data, we need to understand in what scenarios our test should pass and fail. In this case, we should check the following situations:

1. When the array list is empty
2. When the item we are finding is in the array list
3. When the item we are finding is not in the array list

#### Creating Test Cases
In our test class, we need to create several test cases, using the [PexClass] attribute, which allows us to use DataPoint instead of [TestMethod]. We can use the DataPoint to specify several arguments for each test. Here is the code that I am implementing:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[PexMethod]
public void IndexOfTest(List<int> list, int x, int expected)
{
    ArrayList arrList = new ArrayList(list);
    bool inList = (arrList.IndexOf(x) != -1);

    Assert.AreEqual(expected > 0, inList);
    if (expected > 0) Assert.AreEqual(expected, arrList.IndexOf(x));
}
```
In this test case, we pass three arguments to the IndexOfTest method: A List of integer data, an integer x, and an integer expected. We create an ArrayList object and load it with our list data.

At the point when we execute our test, our PexLauncher will automatically generate all possible inputs.

#### Running the Test
The results are then displayed in the output window. We should see a green line if our test passed and a red line if our test failed.

### Conclusion
This lab is about designing and performing automatic tests using Microsoft FxCop and Microsoft Pex, a tool designed to test .NET assemblies for static code analysis and generating input values for unit testing. Testing indexing functions using Pex can confirm the correctness of these functions and help us to understand their possible edge cases. This is a great approach to ensure that our program’s functionality and safety and as a result, reliability in any testing environment like a Microsoft Visual Studio.
